proof.p:
We know already that \lim_{x \to 0^+} \frac{1}{x} = \infty and \lim_{x \to 0^-} \frac{1}{x} = -\infty. Consider

                \lim_{x \to 0^+} \frac{1}{x^2} \amp = 
                \lim_{x \to 0^+} \left(\frac{1}{x}\right)^2 \amp = \amp \text{algebra}
                \left(\lim_{x \to 0^+} \frac{1}{x}\right)^2 \amp = \infty \text{ exponent property}

            Notice that these steps would not change if we approaches 0 from the left because the square would eliminate the negatives. Thus because the limit is defined and has the same result from the left and right then the limit (two sided) is also defined.

subsubsection.p:
What can we do if we want to calculate the derivative of a sum of functions, i.e., f(x)+g(x)? We can try using the definition.

			\frac{d(f(x)+g(x))}{dx} = \amp 
			\lim_{x \to a} \frac{(f(x)+g(x))-(f(a)-g(a))}{x-a} = \amp \text{ definition}
			\lim_{x \to a} \frac{(f(x)-f(a))+(g(x)-g(a))}{x-a} = \amp \text{ commutative property}
			\lim_{x \to a} \frac{f(x)-f(a)}{x-a}+\frac{g(x)-g(a)}{x-a} = \amp \text{ algebraic property}
			\lim_{x \to a} \frac{f(x)-f(a)}{x-a}+ \lim_{x \to a} \frac{g(x)-g(a)}{x-a} = \amp \text{ limit summation property}
			\frac{d f(x)}{dx}+ \frac{d g(x)}{dx} = \amp \text{ definition}

What can we do if we want to calculate the derivative of a scalar times a function, i.e., k \cdot f(x)? Use the definition again.
\frac{d k f(x)}{dx} = \amp \lim_{x \to a} \frac{k f(x)- k f(a)}{x-a} = \amp \text{the definition}
Finish this proof. Remember the example above.

example.p:
We calculate the derivative of f(x)=\sin(x)+x^2\cos(x).

				f^\prime(x) & = \frac{d \sin(x)}{dx} + \frac{d x^2\cos(x)}{dx} & & \text{sum property}
				 & = \cos(x) + \frac{d x^2\cos(x)}{dx} & & \text{known form}
				 & = \cos(x) + \frac{d x^2}{dx}\cos(x)+x^2\frac{d \cos(x)}{dx} & & \text{product property}
				 & = \cos(x) + 2x\cos(x)+x^2(-\sin(x)) & & \text{known forms}
				 & = \cos(x) + 2x\cos(x)-x^2\sin(x)

solution.p:
Note how each part of the chain rule is calculated and assembled.

				h(x) = \amp \sqrt{\color{red}{x^2-1}}
				f(u) = \amp \sqrt{u}. 
				\color{red}{g(x)} = \amp \color{red}{x^2-1}. 
				f^\prime(u) = \amp \frac{1}{2}u^{-\frac{1}{2}}. 
				\color{red}{g^\prime(x)} = \amp \color{red}{2x}. 
				h^\prime(x) = \amp f^\prime({\color{red}{g(x)}})\color{red}{g^\prime(x)}
				 = \amp \frac{1}{2}({\color{red}{x^2-1}})^{-\frac{1}{2}}\color{red}{2x} 
				 = \amp \frac{x}{\sqrt{x^2-1}}.

example.p:
Calculate the first derivative.

			g^\prime(y) = \amp \frac{(y+2)(2y-4)-(y^2-4y)(1)}{(y+2)^2} 
			 = \amp \frac{2y^2-8-y^2+4y}{(y+2)^2}
			 = \amp \frac{y^2+4y-8}{(y+2)^2}.

		Because this is a rational function it is undefined where there is division by zero. This is still just y=-2. Next we determine where it is zero. Because this is a rational function that is solely where the numerator is zero. It does not factor, so we apply the quadratic formula.

			y = \amp \frac{-(-4) \pm \sqrt{(-4)^2-4(1)(-8)}}{2(1)}
			 = \amp \frac{4 \pm \sqrt{48}}{2}
			 = \amp \frac{4 \pm 4\sqrt{3}}{2}
			 = \amp 2 \pm 2\sqrt{3}

Calculate the second derivative.

			g''(y) = \amp \frac{(y+2)^2(2y+4)-(y^2-4y-8)(2[y+2]^1 \cdot 1)}{(y+2)^4}
			 = \amp \frac{2y^3+12y^2+24y+16-(2y^3+12y^2-32)}{(y+2)^4}
			 = \amp \frac{24y+48}{(y+2)^4}
			 = \amp \frac{24(y+2)}{(y+2)^4}
			 = \amp \frac{24}{(y+2)^3}.

		Because this is a rational function this is undefined where there is division by zero which is still just y=-2. The second derivative never equals zero because the numerator is constant.

We want to calculate \int_0^{10} 15x^2-4x+1 \; dx. Because we do not know off the top of our heads a function for which this is the derivative, we will need to use the properties.

			\int_0^{10} 15x^2-4x+1 \; dx = & 
			\int_0^{10} 15x^2 \; dx + \int_0^{10} -4x \; dx + \int_0^{10} 1 \; dx = & \text{ sum property}
			\int_0^{10} 5 \cdot 3x^2 \; dx + \int_0^{10} -2 \cdot 2x \; dx + \int_0^{10} 1 \; dx = & \text{ turned into known derivatives}
			5 \int_0^{10} 3x^2 \; dx -2 \int_0^{10} 2x \; dx + \int_0^{10} 1 \; dx = & \text{ scalar product property (twice)}
			5 \left( \left. x^3 \right|_0^{10} \right) -2 \left( \left. x^2 \right|_0^{10} \right) + \left. x \right|_0^{10}  = & \text{ FTC}
			5(10^3-0^3)-2(10^2-0^2)+(10-0) = & 4810

Sometimes the integrand is not quite the derivative of a function, but we can adjust a coefficient to make it work.

			\int_1^5 x^4 \; dx & =
			\int_1^5 \frac{1}{5} \cdot 5 x^4 \; dx & = \text{ insert needed coefficient}
			\frac{1}{5} \int_1^5 5 x^4 \; dx & = \text{ scalar product property}
			\frac{1}{5} \left. x^5 \right|_1^5 & = \text{ FTC}
			\frac{1}{5} \left( 5^5-1^5 \right) & = 3124.

		This technique if frequently used.

solution.p:
We need to calculate \theta^{\prime \prime} to find extrema for the rate of tipping of the mirror. This will be easier if we re-write the model in terms of the variable time. We know b=1000-14.7t The angle of the mirror is \theta = \arctan\left(\frac{1995}{1000-14.7t}\right).

				\theta^\prime & = \frac{1}{1+\left(\frac{1995}{1000-14.7t}\right)^2} \cdot -\frac{1995}{(1000-14.7t)^2}(-14.7)
				 & = \frac{58653}{9960050-58800t+432.18t^2}
				\theta^{\prime \prime} & = \frac{8.621991000\times 10^11 - 2.534865354\times 10^10 \times t + 1.863126035\times 10^8 \times t^2}{(4980025-29400t+216.09t^2)^2(1000-14.7t)}

			Extrema can occur where the second derivative is zero or undefined. There are no zeros. It is undefined at t=1000/14.7 which is when \theta=\pi/2. This is when the mirror is pointed straight up. Testing \theta^\prime(1000/14.7) to \theta^\prime(1001/14.7) indicates this is a maximum.

subsection.p:
	x = \amp \sinh y. 
	 = \amp \frac{e^y-e^{-y}}{2} 
	 = \amp \frac{e^y-e^{-y}}{2} \cdot \frac{e^y}{e^y} 
	 = \amp \frac{e^{2y}-1}{2e^y}. 
	2xe^y = \amp e^{2y}-1. 
	e^{2y}-2xe^y-1 = \amp 0. 
	u^2-2xu-1 = \amp 0.
	u = \amp \frac{-(-2x)+\sqrt{(-2x)^2-4(1)(-1)}}{2(1)} 
	 = \amp \frac{2x+\sqrt{4x^2+4}}{2} 
	 = \amp \frac{2x+2\sqrt{x^2+1}}{2} 
	 = \amp x+\sqrt{x^2+1}.
	e^y = \amp x+\sqrt{x^2+1}.
	\ln(e^y) = \amp \ln(x+\sqrt{x^2+1}).
	y = \amp \ln(x+\sqrt{x^2+1}).

Why was the \pm left out of the quadratic solution? Because e^y is always positive, so that would not provide a real solution for us.

	y = \amp \ln(x+\sqrt{x^2+1}).
	y^\prime = \amp \frac{1}{x+\sqrt{x^2+1}} \cdot \left( 1+\frac{1}{2}(x^2+1)^{-1/2}(2x) \right)
	 = \amp \frac{1+x(x^2+1)^{-1/2}}{x+\sqrt{x^2+1}}
	 = \amp \frac{1+x(x^2+1)^{-1/2}}{x+\sqrt{x^2+1}} \cdot \frac{\sqrt{x^2+1}}{\sqrt{x^2+1}}
	 = \amp \frac{\sqrt{x^2+1}+x}{(x+\sqrt{x^2+1})\sqrt{x^2+1}}
	 = \amp \frac{1}{\sqrt{x^2+1}}.

Notice in what ways this is similar to the derivative of inverse sine function and in which ways it is different.
